    rule1Title: "1. Acoustic Cavitation & Microjet Erosion Control (Rayleigh-Plesset Thresholds)",
    rule1Text: "When fluid accelerates through a 5–25 μm annular valve orifice at velocities exceeding 400 m/s, static pressure plummets below the vapor pressure of the solvent, generating dense clouds of vapor cavitation bubbles. As the fluid decelerates across the impact ring, static pressure instantly recovers, forcing violent asymmetrical bubble collapse. This produces concentrated liquid microjets with localized shockwave pressures exceeding 10,000 bar (1.0 GPa) impacting the valve seat at sonic velocities. Always specify micro-grain tungsten carbide (WC-Co 6%) or hot isostatically pressed silicon nitride (Si3N4) for operating pressures above 1,000 bar to resist mechanical fatigue spalling and micro-cratering.",
    rule2Title: "2. Viscous Dissipation & Adiabatic Temperature Rise Management",
    rule2Text: "Under high-pressure homogenization, nearly 100% of mechanical pumping work is dissipated as thermal energy within the micro-orifice zone via intense viscous shear friction. The adiabatic temperature increase follows the thermodynamic relation ΔT = ΔP / (ρ · Cp), equating to approximately +2.5°C to +3.2°C per 100 bar for aqueous and dairy fluids. At 1,500 bar, product temperature surges by +38°C to +45°C in milliseconds. To prevent whey protein thermal denaturation, Maillard pigment browning, and bioactive nutrient degradation, install immediate downstream sanitary multi-tube scraped-surface or plate chillers operating at <4°C.",
    rule3Title: "3. Hydraulic Pulsation Dampening & Nitrogen Bladder Integrity",
    rule3Text: "Positive displacement triplex and quintuplex reciprocating plunger pumps induce inherent sinusoidal flow and pressure pulsations (up to ±30% without dampening). A properly charged nitrogen bladder accumulator absorbs hydraulic inertia shockwaves. Maintain nitrogen pre-charge at exactly 60% to 70% of continuous operating pressure. If the bladder loses charge or ruptures, pressure oscillations spike past ASME BPVC vessel design ratings, triggering fatigue cracking in cylinder blocks, premature packing extrusion, and severe valve seat chattering.",
    rule4Title: "4. 3-A Sanitary Standard 04-05 & CIP Sanitization Protocol",
    rule4Text: "Per 3-A Sanitary Standard 04-05 (Homogenizers and Reciprocating Energy Pumps), all product-contact surfaces must maintain a surface finish of Ra ≤ 0.8 μm (32 μin) with zero dead-legs or unvented crevices. Valve seats experiencing cavitation pitting deeper than 25 μm harbor biofilms and must be immediately removed for diamond-lapping or replacement. Ensure CIP detergent velocities exceed 1.5 m/s across the valve seat assembly during cleaning cycles, and inspect plunger seal water-flush channels continuously to prevent microbial ingress.",
